<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Raichur</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1520">1520</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raichur">Raichur</a> was fought in 1520 between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishnadevaraya">Krishnadevaraya</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adil-shahi-dynasty">Bijapur Sultanate</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-adil-shah">Ismail Adil Shah</a> near the fortress of Raichur in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>. Krishnadevaraya's victory gave him Raichur and control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raichur-doab">Raichur Doab</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> and Tungabhadra rivers. The Portuguese chronicler Fernão Nunes left a detailed account of the campaign.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-raichur.jpg" alt="Battle of Raichur" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Madhu B M · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Raichur_Fort_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The fort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raichur">Raichur</a> was built by the Kakatiya king Prataparudra in 1294 and was disputed for nearly two centuries afterwards. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bin-tughluq">Muhammad bin Tughluq</a> captured it in 1323 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a> in 1347, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saluva-narasimha">Saluva Narasimha Deva Raya</a> later failed to retake it. The immediate cause of the war came in 1520, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishnadevaraya">Krishnadevaraya</a> sent Seyed Maraikar, a Muslim in his service, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a> with a large sum to buy horses. Maraikar instead went over to Adil Khan with the money, and Krishnadevaraya's demand for his return was refused.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Vijayanagara army, under its chief commander Saluva Timmarusu, had 32,600 cavalry and 551 elephants by contemporary reckoning, with Pemmasani Ramalinga Nayaka leading the vanguard; its infantry is said to have numbered over 700,000, though writers disagree on the numbers of foot on both sides. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adil-shah">Adil Shah</a> came to relieve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raichur">Raichur</a> with a much smaller army but a strong advantage in artillery. He crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna-river">Krishna River</a> and advanced on the Vijayanagara camp.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishnadevaraya">Krishnadevaraya</a>'s first attack was thrown back in disorder by heavy artillery fire, but he rallied his officers for a counter-charge that drove the Bijapuris into panic and retreat. The Vijayanagara army lost over 16,000 dead; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a> lost Mirza Jehangir and had five captains, among them Salabut Khan, taken prisoner. Krishnadevaraya took the Bijapuri camp with 100 elephants and 400 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a>, then returned to the siege. Portuguese arquebusiers under Cristovão de Figueiredo picked off defenders from the walls, and after its governor was killed the garrison surrendered.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adil-shah">Adil Shah</a> failed to appear for a meeting at Mudgal to pay homage, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishnadevaraya">Krishnadevaraya</a> occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a>, and after being deceived by the ambassador Asad Khan Lari he invaded again, winning battles at Sagar, Shorapur and Kemba. His attempt to set up a Bahmani prince as king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> only hardened the hostility of the five <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultans</a>, and the victory pushed them towards alliance against Vijayanagara. Historians reject the older reading of the battle as a clash of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> and Muslims. Richard Eaton argues that the win led Vijayanagara to undervalue military technology while the Sultans kept improving theirs.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Raichur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The empire's greatest commander was Krishnadevaraya (r. 1509–1529), who defeated the Gajapati rulers of Odisha in campaigns between 1513 and 1519, taking Udayagiri and Kondavidu, and in 1520 defeated Ismail Adil Shah of Bijapur at the <b>Battle of Raichur</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
